## What to deliver

Build and rig the garrison up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a low, wide fortified blockhouse with a crenellated
parapet and amber-glowing firing ports (the fixed body), a front muster gate with a
hinged deployment ramp, and a belfry over the gate holding a swinging bell. **Author the
two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands:
`muster_ramp_drop` (the ramp hinging down from the gate's sill toward the ground and
lifting back, eased so it carries weight rather than sliding linearly) and
`muster_bell_swing` (the bell swinging side to side about its yoke like a pendulum). The
animations are the fixed contract — produce both, by those names, both self-playing and
looping; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. Before you finish, run
`voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from —
an unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one
part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that
instant of an animation to check the motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded
per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
